# Implement read-only mailbox workflows

## Goal

Ship useful read access before any mailbox mutation is enabled.

## Required work

- Implement mailbox and label listing.
- Implement paginated message and thread search.
- Implement message, header, and attachment retrieval.
- Implement conversation retrieval with explicit mapping between Proton
  conversations and provider-neutral threads.
- Bound body and attachment sizes and sanitize filenames.
- Preserve stable identifiers across pagination and reconnects.

## Current evidence

- Mailbox/label catalog metadata can be captured passively and retained as
  immutable in-memory snapshots, then paged with authenticated
  account/generation/query-bound cursors without rereading Proton during
  continuation. Public transport DTO fields remain undefined by the frozen
  contract, so no wire shape is invented.
- Exact single-message `mail/v4/messages/{messageID}` responses can now be
  reduced to bounded identity/envelope/read-state/label/MIME and attachment
  descriptor metadata while dropping body, headers, passwords, cryptographic
  packets, and attachment bytes. The managed browser can passively wait for the
  next app-owned detail GET without clicking, navigating, reloading, or
  injecting
  a provider call, with clean timeout and ambiguity handling. This still does
  not satisfy targeted `get_message`: current WebClients may use cached message
  body without a network fetch, and plaintext body/header plus attachment
  decryption evidence remains open.

- Provider-neutral attachment output now has pure safety primitives before any
  filesystem access. Untrusted provider filenames are reduced to one portable
  UTF-8 leaf capped at 255 bytes, path separators/control characters are
  neutralized, leading-dot and Windows device-name hazards are removed, and
  diagnostics redact the resulting filename.
- Caller attachment destinations normalize only when strictly relative and
  reject parent traversal, absolute roots, backslashes, control characters, and
  drive/alternate-stream colon syntax. Inline eligibility uses the frozen
  `32 KiB` contract ceiling exactly.
- The local runtime now owns bounded file output under an explicit absolute
  root. It opens every root/destination directory component through held file
  descriptors with no-follow semantics, enforces the configured byte ceiling
  before creating output, writes a private `0600` `.partial` inode, and commits
  with an atomic hard link that never overwrites an existing entry. Synthetic
  tests cover nested output, root and destination symlinks, later root-path
  replacement, existing file/symlink refusal, missing parents, oversized bytes,
  private mode, single-link cleanup, and redacted diagnostics. This boundary is
  not yet wired to a public attachment tool because plaintext browser retrieval
  remains unresolved.

- Exact attachment metadata responses now have a separate bounded projector for
  `GET /api/mail/v4/attachments/{attachment_id}/metadata`. It requires provider
  success, binds body `Attachment.ID` to the requested attachment, and binds
  `Attachment.MessageID` to the caller-expected parent message before retaining
  only sanitized filename, declared size, and MIME type. A target-session CDP
  classifier tracks only exact metadata GET lifecycles, accepts only HTTP-200
  JSON with stable attachment identity, discards failed app retries, and caps
  unfinished matching requests at 16. Sender/address data, conversation
  identity, key packets, signatures, request headers, and cookies are discarded.
- Current WebClients fetches encrypted attachment bytes and full metadata in
  parallel, derives the attachment session key, then calls `decryptAndVerify`
  client-side. Therefore the metadata projector proves message-scoped identity
  and safe metadata only; it does not make encrypted HTTP bytes equivalent to
  the plaintext `attachment_v1` response.

- Header research found two distinct fields on the exact message-detail
  response. `Message.Header` is raw header text, while `Message.ParsedHeaders`
  maps names to either one string or an array of strings; WebClients reads the
  parsed form for message semantics and combines raw `Header` with
  MIME-generated
  headers when exporting a message. The frozen contract has no standalone
  `get_headers` capability and only says `message_v1` contains headers requested
  by the caller. Its transport DTO/header-selection and normalization shape is
  still undefined, so the adapter continues to discard both fields rather than
  inventing casing, duplicate-value ordering, or raw-versus-parsed semantics.

- Rendered-body research confirms the opened message article exposes the
  provider
  ID through `data-message-id` and its same-origin content iframe exposes
  `#proton-root`. That is not yet a complete message-body source: WebClients can
  remove collapsed quoted content from the iframe DOM, applies renderer/search
  transforms, and marks unread messages read after expansion/body load. A future
  body projection must remain passive and prove completeness before mapping this
  surface to provider-neutral message body semantics.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every path is classified read-only by the tool contract.
- Search, pagination, Unicode, MIME, and malformed-message cases are tested.
- Attachment traversal and oversized outputs fail safely.
- Logs contain identifiers and timing only, never content.
- The primary web UI adapter passes every read workflow; no paid Bridge or
  direct-API client identity is an unstated prerequisite.

## Evidence

- Synthetic mailbox fixtures
- Contract and adapter integration tests
